## 4. QTL settings and analysis sequence

The first part imports a selfed RIL cross (`riself`), fills missing genotypes by the most likely genotype sequence, counts inferred crossovers, estimates recombination fractions and re-estimates the map. The updated cross is exported with the prefix `CG104_after_fill2` and reloaded in the second part. This prefix is retained from the supplied code; it is an output label and should be adapted to the actual population if necessary.

| Setting | Supplied value |
| :--- | :--- |
| Cross type | Selfed recombinant inbred lines, `riself` |
| Genotype filling | `argmax` |
| Map-estimation error probability | `0.0001` |
| Map-estimation function | Kosambi |
| CIM method | Haley–Knott regression, `hk` |
| Marker covariates | `3` |
| CIM exclusion window | `5 cM` |
| CIM error-probability argument | `0.0001` |
| CIM map-function argument | Kosambi |
| Permutations | `1000` per analyzed phenotype |
| Significance level | `0.05` per phenotype |
| Parallel permutation workers | Up to `60`, constrained by detected cores and the R `mc.cores` option |

Only numeric phenotype columns are retained. For each trait, the script permutes phenotype values, reruns CIM and records the maximum LOD across the scan. These maxima define a genome-wide threshold for that trait; this procedure does not additionally correct across all traits.

Peak extraction uses the external `define.peak1()` function. Without its source, the peak-selection and interval-definition method cannot be specified from this script alone. The code then fits each candidate QTL individually and extracts `result.full[1, 5]` as PVE. It estimates additive effects separately using `sim.geno()` and `effectscan()` and filters peaks against the trait-specific threshold.

